#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05970e26-5821-56d5-9192-cd54ac992296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 5,\\,-9\\le y\\le -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336077} {:answer/id #uuid "66a3960b-62dc-53fd-84aa-3678e0fb86aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 13,\\,3\\le y\\le 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336947} {:answer/id #uuid "712b9c6e-788b-5ba0-8229-df8fca209a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\le x\\le 6,\\,-13\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337040} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5bbdd7-51a4-577f-a287-db618f622b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 5,\\,-7\\le y\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337327} {:answer/id #uuid "fa563dbb-82b3-57bc-8de5-0041f39c1575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\le x\\le 0,\\,1\\le y\\le 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "712b9c6e-788b-5ba0-8229-df8fca209a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\le x\\le 6,\\,-13\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337040} :answer-field/id #uuid "f957fa19-2286-54e9-bed6-f7be3613a470" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fb3f2b5-56e5-553e-8562-5d6280836898" :question/math-academy-id "q-70523" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -7,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1+5 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5\\le 1+5 \\\\ 4 & \\le x\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $4\\le x\\le 6.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -6 & \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 6 \\\\ -6-7 & \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -7\\le 6-7 \\\\ -13 & \\le y\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-13\\le y\\le -1.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $4\\le x\\le 6$ and $-13\\le y\\le -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41fcb26e-2f76-5ee2-a2c2-c8efd41ac9e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336653} {:answer/id #uuid "7946acd5-7bfa-57bf-b14e-d6a7d280f21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337121} {:answer/id #uuid "7add0c89-bf18-542a-917b-05f9444ca0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337140} {:answer/id #uuid "a4225c05-b804-5f98-ae97-c055c7d84dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{20}+{y}^{2}=20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337524} {:answer/id #uuid "cf082d19-11d6-58c7-9646-555d894a03c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{20}-{y}^{2}=20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7add0c89-bf18-542a-917b-05f9444ca0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337140} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef356438-0ae0-5bfd-a254-1589c91ed1e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47f058ff-7b94-5522-94e5-568b71081b68" :question/math-academy-id "q-71192" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $R(3,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=20-{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $R$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,2) & =20-(3)^{2}-2(2)^{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $R(3,2)$ is the $3$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ 20-{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $R(3,2)$ is the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=17.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "379edbc4-958b-59eb-bdc5-5897f81d1e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2=4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336550} {:answer/id #uuid "3bbf9aff-6ec4-5ead-8cb9-70f1e2c12474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336594} {:answer/id #uuid "93f41eb2-bc74-558f-a139-bec3b72aa513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337384} {:answer/id #uuid "e68d2210-b853-5c64-a844-85c31e696235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}-{x}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338045} {:answer/id #uuid "e88e5fd5-cf85-57c8-b1a6-37efdcf80aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x=\\pm (y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93f41eb2-bc74-558f-a139-bec3b72aa513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337384} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b1487de-32bb-570f-8c7f-10e1855abb71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb2a5bfe-54ec-5329-9de1-bfb5261e1c81" :question/math-academy-id "q-71213" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $V(2,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{8y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $V$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2) & =\\frac{8(2)}{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $V(2,2)$ is the $2$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{8y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =2 \\\\ 4y & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4y+4 & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $V(2,2)$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97b5337a-3e06-5fca-8dee-9ccb9bb3e791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467842} {:answer/id #uuid "ca10f725-532e-52f8-b216-80660df1aff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97b5337a-3e06-5fca-8dee-9ccb9bb3e791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467842} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f26b394-09a9-58cd-8127-d23fbca6af8c" :answer-field/key "$c=3$ gives a hyperbola" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468642} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "572c0f09-8cda-57e4-84ee-e9bf11b0969a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467844} {:answer/id #uuid "3debfd10-6252-58a8-80d0-0fe4ad6b3d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3debfd10-6252-58a8-80d0-0fe4ad6b3d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467845} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4b044d1-ec5c-5118-ba8c-99f2a5e9c147" :answer-field/key "$c=0$ gives an ellipse" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468643} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d92d5ae0-bdbf-5aac-a192-d4634abd4820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467846} {:answer/id #uuid "80aced54-71e3-53cc-8d38-ae55e211374c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80aced54-71e3-53cc-8d38-ae55e211374c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467847} :answer-field/id #uuid "7551102b-2721-527c-a9e6-1c6948d2bf34" :answer-field/key "$c=1$ gives a circle" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e04ff7ee-ed94-5861-b7e8-a3f79254490c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71391" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4}{{y}^{2}}$, clear the denominator in $f(x,y)=c$ and classify the resulting conic equation in the entire $xy$-plane, including points excluded from the function's domain. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4}{{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-4 & =c{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(3-c){y}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=3$ then the level curves are $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2},$ which consists of two vertical lines.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=4,$ which is an ellipse.\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2,$ which is a circle.\n\nSo, only statements “$c=0$ gives an ellipse” and “$c=1$ gives a circle” are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a0a2c4b-1d93-5837-bae6-4a3c28e16089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467848} {:answer/id #uuid "14a75206-caba-527a-ab7a-6a2b9a8d8748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a0a2c4b-1d93-5837-bae6-4a3c28e16089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467848} :answer-field/id #uuid "19c95e90-566c-5c73-bd5c-bb5cbff441a5" :answer-field/key "$c=1$ gives a line" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468645} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb7223c5-838d-5ef7-859b-ec117f5a0b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467850} {:answer/id #uuid "83112c20-89a5-5790-800e-3e3c93383a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83112c20-89a5-5790-800e-3e3c93383a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467851} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd2bacc0-d212-57b2-89ee-6c74f13e09de" :answer-field/key "$c=2$ gives a hyperbola" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468646} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c053cde-7b3e-5844-88a1-fbf51c0cc806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467852} {:answer/id #uuid "0514df20-5bca-51ca-8efc-b76c2c3044a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0514df20-5bca-51ca-8efc-b76c2c3044a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467853} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a2ff425-5344-55a6-a715-3662f57b95c1" :answer-field/key "$c=0$ gives two lines" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b94a6f8a-60a9-5806-9e18-4814d39c5835" :question/math-academy-id "q-71395" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}-2{y}^{2} & ={c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=1$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=1,$ which is not a line.\n\n- If $c=2$ then the level curve is ${x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=4,$ which is a hyperbola.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $y=\\pm \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}},$ which consists of two lines.\n\nSo, only statements “$c=2$ gives a hyperbola” and “$c=0$ gives two lines” are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25c6804e-8060-5dff-bba1-acaf4df961ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(8x-7)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524452} {:answer/id #uuid "a07a2ebe-3a0b-54c5-a1a1-e83b39b74dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(8x-7)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524636} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e6700f-f254-59c8-947f-7f6a658e28f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(16x-7)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524655} {:answer/id #uuid "d56bf506-fd8c-5071-aec9-cd4f69c5a6e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(16x-7)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524738} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2fea0e-928b-58ba-8851-272406927771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(8x-7)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25c6804e-8060-5dff-bba1-acaf4df961ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(8x-7)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524452} :answer-field/id #uuid "37b56870-5595-52b0-ae23-7f96f4a87f21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "217bdf0c-2bc6-585f-ac52-fad4aec9092f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71954" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(8x-7)}^{4},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=8x-7$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{4}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{4})^{′}=4{g}^{3}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(8x-7)^{′}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (8) \\\\  & =32(8x-7)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d2f6517-e258-513a-b912-2250e7c54e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2+5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336461} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2c8729-231c-5b49-a873-246a32aa4645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2-5x{)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336588} {:answer/id #uuid "77d335ef-89db-5392-8e96-2e5663d37f72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(2+5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337111} {:answer/id #uuid "84c34e15-f7f7-51bd-91ad-be1849bcac42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(2-5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337229} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6755f1-59bc-5601-b289-66317d2b0d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2-5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba6755f1-59bc-5601-b289-66317d2b0d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(2-5x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337682} :answer-field/id #uuid "20dca297-5492-547a-957c-959f9e7520f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ccad926b-6344-5bec-99bc-507d50cda1e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-71956" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(2-5x)}^{3},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2-5x$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{3}{)}^{′}=3{g}^{2}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2-5x{)}^{′}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-15(2-5x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "733d9013-5828-5e8a-a6ca-98d0ee84db3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340151} {:answer/id #uuid "9e08cb06-fa94-559a-b45f-0247593ae993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340553} {:answer/id #uuid "ce427bad-7fbf-553d-94e7-4eac1f2a458e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340960} {:answer/id #uuid "d512d53f-d1ef-5fcf-a459-a5ecee4d6396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341012} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb559b7-bf25-5a4d-8461-b1784d7b4dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d512d53f-d1ef-5fcf-a459-a5ecee4d6396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341012} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c77d87a-34f1-5f64-8f04-06a22766d6be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b1e203a-fce6-56f0-bd3a-5a969f3f611d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72711" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{cos}^{3}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{cos}^{3}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{cos}^{3}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d5cf9e-61a5-56dc-ab85-48cc3361b6cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339348} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4a8bfb-24ab-5d4f-85c0-b03c7be1078c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339502} {:answer/id #uuid "5af65e91-2dda-5a02-a6b9-84e591e12cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339956} {:answer/id #uuid "94adee08-1ae4-5711-87c2-0b92afcedb4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340463} {:answer/id #uuid "a2128603-79a4-5ac2-aad8-d0e79ce06174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94adee08-1ae4-5711-87c2-0b92afcedb4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340463} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ffbbed8-ad51-5238-b319-cf240d8126f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "508dcfe6-ec7a-5fa0-ab23-63e62cd88ad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-72712" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46e32204-b57f-51f4-9028-656492f4466e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339756} {:answer/id #uuid "4b83938a-b3d9-50a4-a9ef-f655cf87e947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339806} {:answer/id #uuid "61a071b8-8278-5181-97b3-31ae95cf4439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339999} {:answer/id #uuid "7e365f2c-7be7-5a6c-bb21-4f90b3aae003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340266} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a927e5-02b7-5789-b37b-2831c6b2b5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61a071b8-8278-5181-97b3-31ae95cf4439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339999} :answer-field/id #uuid "446108f8-7f35-5931-91d5-61b1fc42e37e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dce072f1-1bfb-5e57-9eb5-e624cb48ce45" :question/math-academy-id "q-72715" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the second term using the definition of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nWe put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b427be-d9bc-5ed9-b80c-87563fdf7c76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339185} {:answer/id #uuid "1438b660-b2ac-58bb-a729-36647cc32743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339308} {:answer/id #uuid "60430fb6-dfb4-5549-8924-d5dd7de854cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339987} {:answer/id #uuid "a3eb2d6e-a958-549e-aec9-ad122592f612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340615} {:answer/id #uuid "e0448a81-7964-5927-b78f-f1a104854afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1438b660-b2ac-58bb-a729-36647cc32743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339308} :answer-field/id #uuid "eccad489-b369-52f4-9f85-91e58a357c5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "784294da-08c1-5f80-a6f1-add45f71373b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73237" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{5+1}}{5+1}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{6}}{6}-\\frac{(-2)^{6}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{64}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f5ad400-1475-5884-8d73-aa99d98ec4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339542} {:answer/id #uuid "57d0a154-1aaa-5696-835d-3eecfa11a6d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339929} {:answer/id #uuid "86e1fe55-454a-5121-bac4-46896aa3ba50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340340} {:answer/id #uuid "908c7900-9735-5a67-b556-85b9b49edab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340424} {:answer/id #uuid "f836db60-b44d-5c4b-8a95-1f0a325c7eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f836db60-b44d-5c4b-8a95-1f0a325c7eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341322} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd6a14fe-e999-58b2-a558-e4c85c9c9d40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03c3f067-95a3-5c61-bbcc-2fdbc5e2a97b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73457" :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨1,-3,2⟩,$ $b=⟨2,-1,-1⟩,$ and $c=⟨3,-3,0⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-3))i-(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 3)j+(2\\cdot (-3)-(-1)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-3i-3j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨1,-3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,-3,-3⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51082891-6c1b-52bb-b8ef-b6a007b87b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339869} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa28b6a-dcb5-5ac6-b93d-070512ac799c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340223} {:answer/id #uuid "7d205186-6df2-56a2-865f-bcd95b4af7a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340256} {:answer/id #uuid "819cb18c-aae0-5874-97f5-c6cb16234ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340294} {:answer/id #uuid "d59c3afe-dcd0-50af-abf3-3623837dac03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d205186-6df2-56a2-865f-bcd95b4af7a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340256} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1370dba-ea66-512a-8e16-f181dabee801" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a3b3ce2-a088-5e9d-b6fc-165f8a2b77d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-73458" :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨-2,4,3⟩,$ $b=⟨-1,3,2⟩,$ and $c=⟨2,-1,4⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 4-2\\cdot (-1))i-((-1)\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2)j+((-1)\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =14i+8j-5k \\\\  & =⟨14,8,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-2,4,3⟩\\cdot ⟨14,8,-5⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 14+4\\cdot 8+3\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4597bbb8-2339-59d7-a89d-84e7d82e4af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339746} {:answer/id #uuid "6ddbdce8-64a8-5b4c-81c7-29ded57a3090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340100} {:answer/id #uuid "7b08f4f2-f8e6-57a8-824a-14ba21be0f46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340227} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2cba1e-d97d-5cb4-a610-06c9a813470e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340542} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bc4d09-f1f6-5163-96f4-651b14c60f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b08f4f2-f8e6-57a8-824a-14ba21be0f46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340227} :answer-field/id #uuid "76e5b949-55b3-5b2f-a023-770c01a2ee78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a18cd55a-1984-588f-a487-c7100ebf414c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73459" :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨-2,0,-1⟩,$ $b=⟨-2,-1,0⟩,$ and $c=⟨3,1,4⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 1)i-((-2)\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 3)j+((-2)\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-4i+8j+k \\\\  & =⟨-4,8,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-2,0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,8,1⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 8+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ec09212-1f8f-5eb2-a230-a2693831de19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339397} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8ab319-3f23-56d1-8521-08a5fcb129e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339659} {:answer/id #uuid "77ce1623-cee4-55da-93c5-1dffe59879a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340196} {:answer/id #uuid "a3546050-0793-53d1-8739-d63260cbe301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340609} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a8a6c3-6266-58a1-8a1e-1cd435ceef17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ec09212-1f8f-5eb2-a230-a2693831de19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339397} :answer-field/id #uuid "6162d8bc-16bb-5616-9c27-caf490510db9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8ba8c6e-ad18-5042-8933-2affd9193629" :question/math-academy-id "q-73461" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨3,-2,1⟩$, $q=⟨2,-1,0⟩$ and $r=⟨1,-2,4⟩.$ Find $q\\cdot (r\\times p).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $q\\cdot (r\\times p)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $q,$ $r,$ and $p.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q\\cdot (r\\times p) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2((-2)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2))+(1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =12-11 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a8d4cf-2a52-5eb3-932c-de54f969d498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339176} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2029a1-5daa-5d17-915b-5cb3db9d0a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339665} {:answer/id #uuid "7364ef2c-a924-50d5-ae40-c9a6106f8bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340152} {:answer/id #uuid "84d38775-5cfe-5497-a2ea-945dc01057b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340321} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f005ac-dfb9-5a8e-9f29-140e8e17a482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84d38775-5cfe-5497-a2ea-945dc01057b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340321} :answer-field/id #uuid "50438105-46e0-54c5-8329-966f684eb5e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc420fe9-802f-5344-9de7-9b53d8435b37" :question/math-academy-id "q-73462" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨3,1,2⟩$, $q=⟨1,-2,1⟩$ and $r=⟨4,1,-2⟩.$ Find $r\\cdot (q\\times p).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $r\\cdot (q\\times p)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $r,$ $q,$ and $p.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot (q\\times p) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)-(1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3)-2(1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-20+1-14 \\\\  & =-33.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e01e90f-f2c8-529a-8475-ffe098a4dce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339392} {:answer/id #uuid "2d627205-3eda-50f7-a543-2c0b44474b89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339525} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffb819c-5b7a-5670-bb5f-a7ce9ecaa93f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339546} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7e1618-a748-5197-8bc3-fe1f6a14f7a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340086} {:answer/id #uuid "e024f0bd-062c-5cb4-83fb-f88a21111438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e01e90f-f2c8-529a-8475-ffe098a4dce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339392} :answer-field/id #uuid "49b74bdf-7b8e-59e4-a0eb-046497c2e345" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f1bc950-3241-5738-85e5-31b6ac10a3b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-73470" :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (b\\times a)$, if $b\\times c=⟨3,-1,2⟩$ and $a=⟨4,-2,6⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨4,-2,6⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-1)+6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12+2+12 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (b\\times a)=-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "335d0d1c-7aa5-5e41-83dd-2bd696206902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339584} {:answer/id #uuid "45b4e8f6-0254-586e-8b2c-8ca34c9155e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 1,\\,y\\le 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339747} {:answer/id #uuid "760a81e8-5ce1-5b78-9884-f109dafe3e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -1,\\,y\\ge -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340179} {:answer/id #uuid "94d1ef80-cf3c-5620-bee7-53c5c5629fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1,\\,y\\le 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340466} {:answer/id #uuid "e778c967-2eb3-5de6-8c75-2e13c5bc4346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -1,\\,y\\ge -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "335d0d1c-7aa5-5e41-83dd-2bd696206902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339584} :answer-field/id #uuid "043f62a1-3349-567c-aec9-24574a79034c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f5b7d05-a282-5021-9aa5-8966b1c2524c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73623" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t-2}+1,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{3}+2,\\,t\\ge 2,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $\\sqrt{t-2}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\ge 2.$ Consequently, $x=\\sqrt{t-2}+1\\ge 1.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that ${t}^{3}\\ge 8$ for all $t\\ge 2.$ Consequently, $y={t}^{3}+2\\ge 8+2=10.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f6b6688-7ebd-5b25-83b7-5c07ad7c688a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\le x\\le 5,\\,1\\le y\\le 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339270} {:answer/id #uuid "25d8d7af-c8e4-5ebb-980e-a55d3a4cd6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-5\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339454} {:answer/id #uuid "3320f739-78f0-51e9-9081-4e93b3163fc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 4,\\,-1\\le y\\le 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339582} {:answer/id #uuid "94fae60d-72de-52be-8e5a-de4200f76d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 6,\\,-5\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340469} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd74067-cc9b-5848-b108-229097ff8d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-5\\le y\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25d8d7af-c8e4-5ebb-980e-a55d3a4cd6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-5\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339454} :answer-field/id #uuid "b483317f-a4a2-5639-8bcf-ae196dafce5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2f3822c-027f-5b78-99af-6944b4b3bf81" :question/math-academy-id "q-73626" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\ge -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -2 \\\\ -2 & \\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ 1-2 & \\le 1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1+2 \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2-3 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\le 2-3 \\\\ -5 & \\le y\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-5\\le y\\le -1.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-1\\le x\\le 3$ and $-5\\le y\\le -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fcf5022-4812-5cf2-a745-37302fcd5dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le -1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339275} {:answer/id #uuid "4915a118-a0a0-5db9-914a-1721d3b42565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\le x\\le 1,\\,-2\\le y\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339784} {:answer/id #uuid "b457cadc-b9f6-5c2c-9f2c-42f3a633bbd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340764} {:answer/id #uuid "d4206d0e-3e82-51c1-8e8c-9cad7033e6f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-1\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341005} {:answer/id #uuid "e66ef8b5-6026-532a-a34b-a4ed72b384c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b457cadc-b9f6-5c2c-9f2c-42f3a633bbd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340764} :answer-field/id #uuid "c59dee8b-2e72-5994-98ef-02b4aa2af213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbbe4cfc-6544-53d2-868c-0f7ad468266a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73632" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{3}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2+1 & \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1\\le 2+1 \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{3}{2}\\le \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\le y\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $1\\le y\\le 2.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-1\\le x\\le 3$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4df8c281-7720-5de4-bfcb-7ec3f40cd92e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+2){e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342878} {:answer/id #uuid "8964b90d-0fb7-5352-a378-5854b735da72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{5t+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343429} {:answer/id #uuid "90b58700-1443-53b7-900c-fe295b8988e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343503} {:answer/id #uuid "92c59c13-5a91-5d60-9e87-defff709bc93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343520} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e0ad1c-9242-5e5e-8095-61f3d7da1e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\sqrt{26t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90b58700-1443-53b7-900c-fe295b8988e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343503} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f4fe4ae-1db5-5267-909a-483c3e60cca6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d30373c-bec9-57f2-8b55-adb3bc1e9678" :question/math-academy-id "q-76414" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=5x+2$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=5t+2.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,5t+2⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t+2)j \\\\  & =i+5\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(5t+2){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\cdot \\sqrt{26}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0470023d-2f7c-572b-8223-a821e9591b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{124}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342286} {:answer/id #uuid "21e4ce2b-359a-5d4a-b474-cf8acda842ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342519} {:answer/id #uuid "3109d7d4-9f83-52e3-8ef2-13bdd11c2459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342650} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d1ba27-795d-5ca7-bff5-0a3d75d5b66f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343667} {:answer/id #uuid "d10bbab9-4de9-5d18-a90a-7d251ca7ad2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0470023d-2f7c-572b-8223-a821e9591b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{124}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342286} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3ec0e9-f2ac-56af-9dc2-ed440ea409ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2497006d-58cf-53a4-9f8c-593f6d881048" :question/math-academy-id "q-76431" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 4\\sqrt{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 4\\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}\\right)j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}\\right)}{t}=\\frac{t}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{t}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}t\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}$, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}t\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{25}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{3}{|}_{1}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({25}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(125-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d872230-dffa-5440-9150-e6a7484563c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{2}-6\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342745} {:answer/id #uuid "41027de0-afa8-5c2b-8896-f3199a916c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342773} {:answer/id #uuid "679fdec6-f7e1-5541-a399-fb14f4197dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(8\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343120} {:answer/id #uuid "970fe583-040b-5004-90dd-58e93faa89d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(16\\sqrt{2}-6\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343562} {:answer/id #uuid "bad2408b-9fca-5a3a-8105-a9d53cef466b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "679fdec6-f7e1-5541-a399-fb14f4197dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(8\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343120} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc5d8aa7-e05a-504e-8119-e3b3b13e8c8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d9906db-a501-54d2-bbd1-292e69086032" :question/math-academy-id "q-76480" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ $\\,1\\le x\\le \\sqrt[4]{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\frac{1}{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le \\sqrt[4]{2}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{4{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{2}}{{t}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}3{x}^{5}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}$, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{2}^{3/2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{4}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(8\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107d5a2a-4f6f-52c4-a7ea-a3c986f47360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342381} {:answer/id #uuid "12208fda-53a7-5d69-9f6a-dbdffb153e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342399} {:answer/id #uuid "768320a8-bc68-56f3-9b17-f4dad32ac2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343266} {:answer/id #uuid "9381484a-6981-59d3-b123-6f51531c898a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343530} {:answer/id #uuid "bd7ef7a7-f907-538f-b138-4c1ddb333884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "107d5a2a-4f6f-52c4-a7ea-a3c986f47360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342381} :answer-field/id #uuid "20642a04-5ca1-5221-ae4c-b1d1be5781d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06fe26ef-4ec2-5c23-a26d-637e986a7e20" :question/math-academy-id "q-76906" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,4,\\,{t}^{2}⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+0+{(2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t\\ge 0$ for $t\\in [0,2].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2\\sqrt{2}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63bfa53b-e47b-50cc-89fd-5a4e4528fce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343084} {:answer/id #uuid "79ea6bc3-b230-56b5-bb39-3960352c7365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343288} {:answer/id #uuid "87e6f26e-9f09-5844-8065-ef122df54dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343414} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0991c3-27a4-50a9-8460-379ec4e4da59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343628} {:answer/id #uuid "d6cbc97b-38b5-54a3-92bc-84bf1dbbd503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6cbc97b-38b5-54a3-92bc-84bf1dbbd503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344123} :answer-field/id #uuid "60342b90-65d0-553f-8346-360744bd9458" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccf4d567-d105-5c9b-9e19-604b98b9eea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-76908" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2t,\\,2{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}+{(2{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(1+2{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =1+2{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(1+2{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(t+\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3+18-0 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f74f6bc-5e7b-58d7-b7fc-c365ef4e92f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342896} {:answer/id #uuid "57499e39-7bdc-59ad-a99d-360f1fc7b2ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342966} {:answer/id #uuid "6677667e-3442-5a82-af63-c15f1638bd7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343106} {:answer/id #uuid "fd321b38-e06d-5b93-9ae1-a114b299ba87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344462} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf30511-d971-58e6-a9d7-f63d74b0483c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6677667e-3442-5a82-af63-c15f1638bd7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343106} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7fb0837-5c09-5cad-8bec-dfcdb3722f64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f089db80-ac84-508f-a172-79a9d0b01dd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76914" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=⟨2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2},\\,2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\n$$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,3t,\\,4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+(3t)^{2}+{(4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡2t+9{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{t}^{2}+9{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =5t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t\\ge 0$ for $t\\in [0,2].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}\\right)|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5a67bb-0704-5374-80b7-f3c08e8d80a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342601} {:answer/id #uuid "46234d75-8481-586d-a8d6-e8817f41182e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342815} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba3f5f9-1429-57ca-8bd7-30d8b83aa72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343454} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b57ee9-5770-5bad-bc47-e53eff6914ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344087} {:answer/id #uuid "e992c30b-4dd9-5634-88df-cea97771fa79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46234d75-8481-586d-a8d6-e8817f41182e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342815} :answer-field/id #uuid "05a88dee-a59b-585a-bbc1-cb4004578b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50a8400d-aac2-5c82-9900-5410f81b5445" :question/math-academy-id "q-77064" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨10,\\,\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,{t}^{2}-1,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{({t}^{2}-1)}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+1+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+t)|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =9+3-0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345271 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025cfcf4-6c89-537a-b8b1-2e525bd5c2da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}+{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342272} {:answer/id #uuid "18941e5d-726d-59b5-9e31-2ef48340a842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342455} {:answer/id #uuid "47398ece-73e5-5c09-a241-0a1c111f3b99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342828} {:answer/id #uuid "b22dcc61-309a-5bea-8c94-67c83b72f6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343803} {:answer/id #uuid "b5dd1c31-05b4-597a-8db3-8c5b0a9b8a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47398ece-73e5-5c09-a241-0a1c111f3b99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342828} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f6d65f0-36c8-5e82-84e3-897a16a6bbcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aec9fc84-c15b-5e80-be71-3437e74d2edf" :question/math-academy-id "q-77068" :question/problem "![](images/54/54cd1a0532bbdbe76bd81d5ad737f6d3e9f5293d08ea78758865b3b71850e346.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $g(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},\\,2t⟩$ from $t=1$ to $t=e.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${g}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{g}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t}-t,\\,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t}-t)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-2+{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+2+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t}+t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{1}{t}+t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}){|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2})-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}+1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417d6dbd-2ce0-543a-b867-66f46ac5a28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342775} {:answer/id #uuid "4af00b12-c488-528b-8d8e-a83c42ba6f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342854} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6338b2-dd43-5df9-9664-ecfbf4ec9d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343153} {:answer/id #uuid "e29b77b4-101f-5781-976e-2efc7b315a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344231} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e5b481-08c2-5c6e-93f4-60c15385b21b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4af00b12-c488-528b-8d8e-a83c42ba6f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342854} :answer-field/id #uuid "f63e4bae-b451-51c9-adf9-3b579789034f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6edd20b3-8235-5d98-a615-bc507b0983b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-77070" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6de928031610e2aaf55e3dd753449f766e7dc028a6618087e7489224a73ea7d.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t\\ge 0$ for $t\\in [0,2].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right)|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a04d171-7f37-5d9d-a2f8-61194e6e93c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342463} {:answer/id #uuid "2dbc28ff-5d76-56ae-8ecf-64a2fdcb3e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342623} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e002a4-bd25-5f07-8e74-c15da484745e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343691} {:answer/id #uuid "b2aa47f5-56ab-5309-a1ff-ddb6c0030709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343807} {:answer/id #uuid "c97a2a57-f756-5bf4-8ca0-819491201663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a04d171-7f37-5d9d-a2f8-61194e6e93c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342463} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bc43f6a-48ec-5f1d-9fa3-8b18c022ec80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3fbb4ec8-936e-5c4c-830d-f88706e61780" :question/math-academy-id "q-77219" :question/problem "![](images/23/2324d20426a6ef6110714341f256ed3943c66d4068cdb9bd3d1763f8f528f5a1.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨6t,-\\frac{3}{t}-{t}^{3}⟩$\nfrom  $t=1$ to $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{t}-{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}-3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}-3{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{4+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}-2+{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}+2+{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}3(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{3}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3(-\\frac{1}{t}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{t}+{t}^{3}){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(-1+27)-(-3+1) \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
